  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 204 AM CDT Sat Sep 5 2026\n\nThe weakening mid-level low continues to move southwestward towards \nMexico early this morning, ushering moisture into South Texas. Above \nnormal PWATs in the Victoria Crossroads (2.1-2.3\") and elsewhere \naround 2\", will help with the formation of seabreeze convection \nthrough this Labor Day Weekend. Ultimately, coverage looks to be \nbest over the Victoria Crossroads with less coverage further south \nand west. Low rain chances (20-40%) continue Monday and Tuesday, \nmainly due to the afternoon seabreeze. Going into the middle of next \nweek, a mid-level low/trough progresses westward across the Gulf, \nriding the southern periphery of the ridge. This mid-level feature \nwill act as an additional lifting mechanism to the seabreeze, \nincreasing rain chances Wednesday to 30-60%. \n\nTemperatures will continue to remain above normal. However, with the \nincreased chance for rain showers, some locations could see \nincreased cloud cover, thus bringing temperatures more closer to \nnormal temporarily. Otherwise, we will be flirting with heat \nadvisory criteria with heat indices ranging from 105-110 across the \narea through the middle of next week. Nonetheless, a moderate to \nhigh heat risk will be present each afternoon.",
